  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 137 PM CDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nThrough Tuesday: Temperatures will show a slow warming trend but \ncontinued dry conditions with cooler than normal morning lows and \nwarmer than normal afternoon highs are still expected in the short \nterm. Surface ridging from the northeast will continue across the \nregion resulting in a northeast to east wind and keeping the Gulf \nclosed through the period. Ridging aloft will remain over the region \nas well. Together these features will result in decent radiational \ncooling again tonight and near full insolation again Tuesday. The \nresult will be critically low afternoon minimum relative humidity \nmaintaining fire danger concerns. /22/\n\nTuesday Night through Monday...\n\nUpper ridging aloft from the west will help keep weather conditions \nquiet across our CWA Tuesday night. Sky cover will be generally \nclear across much of our area heading into the overnight period, \nwith low to mid cumulus clouds across portions of southwest MS and \nportions of northeast LA pushing from the northwest to the \nsoutheast. Expect nighttime lows to dip into the upper 40s/lower 50s \nareawide. Wednesday will remain quiet under clear skies. Look for \nafternoon highs on Wednesday to peak in the low to mid 80s. Dry and \nquiet conditions are expected Wednesday night with lows once again \nin the upper 40s/lower 50s. \n\nThe rest of the extended period will remain quiet thanks upper \nridging aloft. The overall dry airmass will remain across the \nregion through the remainder of this week with dewpoints mixing into \nthe 40s and 50s each afternoon. Temperatures will continue to \nsteadily warm each day. This warming will be felt more with daytime \nhighs, than with overnight lows. Lows through the period will \ngenerally range from the upper 30s to low 50s. Meanwhile highs on \nFriday will range from the upper 70s to middle 80s. By Saturday, \nmost of the area will see highs peak in the lower 80s to around 90s \nacross much of our area. Portions of east MS and northeast MS will \nsee highs peak in the upper 70s by the weekend.  \n\nThese increasing high temperatures in this dry air mass will result \nin afternoon minimum relative humidity values falling to critically \nlow values, i.e. generally into the 20-30% range.  This, coupled \nwith the extremely dry soil conditions across the area will continue \nto promote at least a \"Limited\" threat for dangerous fire conditions \nacross the forecast area through the period. Please continue to \nmonitor the latest forecast and outlook through the week, as some \ncategorical adjustment could be made to portions of, if not all of, \nthe forecast area. Quiet and dry weather will persist across the \narea heading into the new work week. /CR/",
